course: dictionary
lesson: 3808
title: Forelock: hair, history and a phrase
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A word can have more than one meaning. Forelock has a plain meaning about hair, and an older meaning about how people once showed respect. Context tells you which one is meant. In a horse book, «the forelock» means hair. In a story about workers, «tugged their forelocks» means showing respect.
explain: Look at the words around it. «Brush the forelock» is about grooming hair, but «tug your forelock» is about respect for someone powerful. Ask yourself which meaning fits, then swap it into the sentence to check.
explain: The Ukrainian sentence lists the forelock among Cossack images, and the Badger sentence shows hedgehogs leaving «with much respectful swinging of caps and touching of forelocks». Both are about an old polite way of greeting, so the context points to respect, not just hair.
explain: Phrases can have their own meaning. «Take time by the forelock» does not mean grabbing hair. It means act quickly while there is a chance. If the words seem odd, check whether the whole phrase has a special meaning.
fact: A forelock is a lock, or bunch, of hair that grows just above the forehead, on a person or a horse.
fact: In horse care, the forelock is the part of the mane that falls between a horse's ears onto its face.
fact: «To tug your forelock» (UK, old) means to show too much respect to someone rich or powerful.
fact: «Take time by the forelock» means to act quickly while there is a chance.
fact: In the Badger story, the hedgehogs show respect by «touching of forelocks», an old polite way of greeting your «betters».
exercise: In «The pony shook its forelock out of its eyes», what is a forelock? | A long gate on a farm | A saddle strap | A tail that swishes at flies | *A bunch of hair that grows above the forehead, on a horse
exercise: «The poor workers tugged their forelocks to the lord.» What does this show? | They were angry and shouting | They were cold and needed a hat | They were playing a game | *Too much respect for someone rich and powerful
exercise: «She decided to take time by the forelock and send the letter now.» What does the phrase mean? | Forget about the letter | *Act quickly while there is a chance | Grab a handful of hair | Wait until next year
test: Which phrase means to act quickly? | *Take time by the forelock | Count to ten | Tug your forelock | Brush the forelock
test: In «touching of forelocks», what were the hedgehogs showing? | Hunger | Cold feet | Anger at the Badger | *Respect to their betters
exam: Which sentence uses forelock to mean hair? | «They touched their forelocks to the squire.» | *«The foal's forelock fell into its eyes as it ran.» | «He took time by the forelock and left.» | «She tugged her forelock and bowed low to the duke.»
